<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description="The small, explicit telemetry boundary used by qyl.at.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="reading-shell">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy · qyl.at</span>
      <h1 class="page-title">Measure the page, not the person.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Effective 19 October 2026. qyl.at records a bounded same-origin Core Web Vitals event. The optional Ask AI chat is provided by Tluma, a separate service with its own data practices.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="editorial-section editorial-section--raised">
    <article class="reading-shell docs-content">
      <h2>What is recorded</h2>
      <p>LCP, INP, and CLS values, ratings and changes may be sent with the current pathname, navigation type and browser language. Each measurement includes the temporary metric-instance identifier generated by the Web Vitals library so repeated updates can be distinguished; this is not an account or persistent browser identifier. These records are used to evaluate the site’s real-world loading and interaction quality.</p>

      <h2>What is excluded</h2>
      <p>The event does not include viewport dimensions, device memory, URL queries, page or form content, request or response bodies, arbitrary HTTP headers, credentials, account identity, or MCP tool arguments. Unknown fields are rejected by the Worker before export.</p>

      <h2>Where it goes</h2>
      <p>The browser posts to <code>/_qyl/vitals</code> on this origin. A Cloudflare Worker validates the closed payload and forwards an official OTLP log record to the qyl collector. The collector credential remains server-side. The forwarded record is ordinary collector telemetry and follows the collector’s retention below: 30 days by default (<code>QYL_RETENTION_DAYS</code>). The 28-day figure quoted with the site’s performance evidence is a different thing: the Chrome UX Report’s rolling 28-day field aggregate of Core Web Vitals, which Google computes from Chrome users and which does not read these records.</p>

      <h2>Ask AI by Tluma</h2>
      <p>Pages load the chat launcher script from <a href="https://tluma.ai/">Tluma</a>. The hosted chat loads when you open Ask AI; background chat preloading is disabled. The assistant uses the public <a href="https://github.com/ANcpLua/qyl">qyl repository</a> as its source.</p>
      <p>When you open the chat, the widget shares the current page URL with Tluma. Tluma processes your messages, responses, and session information, and may store browser identifiers for conversation continuity. This is separate from the Core Web Vitals event described above. Do not paste secrets or sensitive production logs. See <a href="https://tluma.ai/privacy-policy">Tluma’s privacy policy</a> for access and retention details.</p>

      <h2>Hosted qyl MCP service</h2>
      <p>The hosted MCP service at <code>https://mcp.qyl.at/mcp</code> lets an authorized client read collector telemetry, create, update and delete annotations and saved queries, and update failure status in the same project. This section covers that service; the website sections above remain applicable to the website. The operator is Alexander Nachtmann (ancplua), Austria, reachable at <a href="mailto:anfh22@outlook.com">anfh22@outlook.com</a>.</p>

      <h3>Data processed and purposes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sign-in and authorization.</strong> Access tokens issued by Auth0 (tenant in the EU region) are checked for issuer, audience, expiry, subject, client and the <code>qyl:read</code> permission on every request, and for the write tools also <code>qyl:write</code> (create, update) or <code>qyl:delete</code> (<code>delete_record</code>). The operator keeps the mapping from your account's subject identifier to the collector project you may read in the server's configuration; it does not travel in tool arguments.</li>
        <li><strong>Telemetry queries.</strong> Tool arguments select traces, spans, logs, recorded metric instruments and sessions. The requested results are returned to your connected client. Telemetry can contain timestamps, service names, diagnostic messages and attributes that were sent to the collector.</li>
        <li><strong>Annotations and saved queries.</strong> When a client calls a write tool on your behalf, the collector stores in your project an annotation's text (at most 2000 characters) its labels and classification, and the trace, span, session or failure it is attached to, or a saved query's name, optional description and filter for traces, logs or metrics. Each carries an identifier, creation and change times and a version tag. Everyone and every client allowed to read the project can read them. For 24 hours after a create call the collector also keeps its idempotency key and a fingerprint of the request body, so a retried call returns the stored result. The MCP server keeps no copy.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure status.</strong> The collector stores a failure's open, resolved or ignored status, optional assignee, ignore deadline, change time and version tag in the authorized project. These records guide triage and do not modify the underlying telemetry. A signed verification handle carries the scope needed to recheck fix evidence without storing a verification job.</li>
        <li><strong>Operational records.</strong> The server exports the tool name, timing, status and error type of each call, with the transport, protocol version and server and execution identifiers, as telemetry to your collector project for diagnostics. A W3C trace context the client sends in the request's <code>_meta</code> becomes the parent of that record. It does not record tool arguments, results or conversation content; the content a write tool is asked to store goes to the collector as described above. The server keeps no OAuth tokens and no copy of the telemetry.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Destinations</h3>
      <p>Requested results go to the MCP client you connected, for example ChatGPT, Claude or Codex, which apply their own data practices. The qyl collector supplies and stores the telemetry, annotations, saved queries and failure status; Auth0 handles sign-in and authorization checks; Railway hosts the MCP service and collector; Cloudflare provides the public edge in front of <code>mcp.qyl.at</code> and <code>api.qyl.at</code>.</p>

      <h3>Retention and controls</h3>
      <p>The collector keeps telemetry — including the Core Web Vitals records above — for 30 days by default (<code>QYL_RETENTION_DAYS</code>) and removes older records in a retention run every 60 minutes (<code>QYL_RETENTION_INTERVAL_MINUTES</code>). An annotation expires under the same window, counted from its last change. A saved query does not expire; it stays until it is deleted with <code>delete_record</code> or on a deletion request. <code>delete_record</code> removes one annotation or saved query and cannot remove telemetry. Failure status can be updated through its tool; an ignore deadline controls when an ignored failure returns to triage. Removing project records does not remove copies a connected client already received. Account and deletion requests go to the contact address above.</p>

      <h2>Source and questions</h2>
      <p>The site and collector are open source. Implementation questions or deletion requests can be raised through the <a href="https://github.com/ANcpLua/qyl.at/issues">qyl.at issue tracker</a> or by mail to <a href="mailto:anfh22@outlook.com">anfh22@outlook.com</a>. The <a href="/support/">support page</a> and the <a href="/terms/">terms</a> describe the hosted service further.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
